import BlueprintPage from '../components/BlueprintPage.astro';
const sections = [
{title:'What anxiety can look like',paragraphs:['Worry, fear, panic, avoidance, physical tension, sleep disruption, and difficulty concentrating can affect daily life. People may notice these experiences in particular settings or across several parts of their routine. Similar symptoms can have different explanations, including physical health, medication effects, stress and other mental-health concerns. An online page cannot identify the cause for an individual.','You can prepare by noting when the concern began, what situations bring it on, what you do to feel safer and what activities have become harder. A few examples from work, study, home or relationships can help a clinician understand the pattern. Include what helps, even if it is only temporary. You do not need to arrive with a diagnosis or a complete written history.']},
{title:'What an assessment may consider',paragraphs:['A clinical conversation may explore the form and timing of the worry or fear, physical sensations, sleep, concentration, avoidance, previous support, current medicines and relevant health history. The clinician may ask how the concern affects everyday functioning and whether there are periods when it feels easier. Further assessment or a medical review may be considered if it would help clarify the picture.','An assessment can help distinguish what needs attention and what remains uncertain. It does not promise a label or a particular outcome. Ask what the clinician is considering, what options might follow and how progress will be reviewed. See <a href="/assessments">assessment information</a> and <a href="/psychiatry">psychiatry consultations</a>.']},
{title:'Possible support',paragraphs:['Depending on assessment, support may include therapy, coping strategies, medical review, follow-up, or referral. No particular modality or outcome is guaranteed. Therapy may help people notice patterns, practise responses and work toward personally meaningful goals. A psychiatric consultation may help when you want a medical perspective or have a question about medication. Some people use both services; others start with one.','Read about <a href="/therapy">clinical psychology and therapy</a> and the <a href="/psychiatrist-vs-psychologist">difference between clinician roles</a>. Ask what an approach involves, who provides it, how fees and duration work, and when the clinician will review whether it is helping. Do not start, stop or change a prescribed treatment based only on online information.']},
{title:'Finding care near Greater Noida',paragraphs:['Mind Grace is located in Gamma II, Greater Noida. Check <a href="/location">directions</a> and <a href="/fees">current fees</a> before you request a visit. If you are travelling from Noida or elsewhere in the NCR, verify current routes and travel time on the day. The clinic can confirm whether an in-person or <a href="/teleconsultation">online consultation</a> is available and suitable.','Use <a href="/book">the appointment request page</a> or contact reception with a general question if you are unsure which service to choose. A request is not confirmed until a date and time are agreed. Share only the basic information needed for coordination. Mind Grace is not an emergency service; for immediate danger call 112 or see <a href="/emergency">urgent-help information</a>.']}
];
